# target module

The `target` module resolves the target machine. It converts host settings or explicit flags into a `TargetInfo` value.

## Types

### `Arch`

Supported CPU architectures.

Values: `x86_64`, `aarch64`, `riscv64`, `x86`, `arm`.

- `pointerWidth(self)`: return 32 or 64.

### `BinaryFormat`

Supported container formats.

Values: `elf`, `coff`, `macho`.

### `TargetInfo`

Complete target specification.

Fields:

- `arch`: `Arch`
- `format`: `BinaryFormat`
- `endian`: `std.builtin.Endian`
- `page_size`: page size in bytes
- `default_entry`: default entry symbol
- `default_output`: default output file name

Methods:

- `detect(opts)`: resolve from parsed options. It prefers explicit emulation, then explicit format, then the host.

## Example

```zig
const target_info = sibl.TargetInfo.detect(options);

std.debug.print("arch={s} format={s} page={d}\n", .{
    @tagName(target_info.arch),
    @tagName(target_info.format),
    target_info.page_size,
});
```

## Notes

- AArch64 ELF uses a 65536 byte page size.
- The default entry and output name come from the target.
- Use this value to pick the layout template with `sibl.templateForTarget`.
